<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    We only need to know which app just came to the front, so window content
    retrieval stays off. The service reads event.packageName and nothing else.

    That is a privacy line, and it is also what keeps the app installable by
    hand. With it off, Android offers a plain switch in the accessibility list
    and Play Protect lets the APK through. Turn it on and the same switch
    becomes "allow this app to have full control of your device", because a
    service that can read the screen can read all of it, and Play Protect starts
    warning about the developer at install time. Measured both ways on the same
    phone with the same signing key, and the flag is the only difference.

    Telling one screen inside an app from another, Reels from the rest of
    Instagram, needs that capability. That is what it would cost.

    packageNames is deliberately not set: we need events from other apps too,
    otherwise we cannot tell "entered Instagram from elsewhere" apart from
    "already scrolling inside Instagram".
-->
<accessibility-service xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:accessibilityEventTypes="typeWindowStateChanged"
    android:accessibilityFeedbackType="feedbackGeneric"
    android:accessibilityFlags="flagDefault"
    android:canRetrieveWindowContent="false"
    android:notificationTimeout="150"
    android:description="@string/service_description" />
